# What never needs to reach a Railway build.
#
# Build phase 4.12. `railway up` indexes the working directory, and the first
# attempt DIED rather than merely wasting bandwidth:
#
#   reference/personal-os-work/Reference-repos/agentmemory-Deep-Dive/agentmemory:
#   IO error ... No such file or directory (os error 2)
#
# `reference/` holds symlinks to read-only reference material outside this
# repository, and at least one target is absent on this machine. The indexer
# follows the link, cannot stat the target, and aborts the whole upload. So
# this file is a correctness fix first and a size optimisation second.

# Symlinked reference material. Never part of a build, and the direct cause of
# the failed upload above.
reference/

# The local virtualenv: 373 MB, and Nixpacks builds its own from
# requirements.txt. Uploading it would be slow and would also be WRONG, since a
# macOS venv cannot run on a Linux container.
venv/
.venv/

# Installed frontend dependencies. `npm ci` reinstalls them from the lockfile
# inside the build, which is the point of committing a lockfile.
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/

# Python bytecode and test caches, all machine-local.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/

# Local secrets. Railway variables are the deploy-time source, per Section 24,
# and .env is gitignored anyway. Listed explicitly rather than relied upon,
# because `railway up` indexes the WORKING DIRECTORY rather than the git index,
# so a gitignored file is not automatically excluded here.
.env

# Documentation, planning artifacts and the tracker. Large, and none of it is
# read at runtime.
docs/
requirements/
tracker/
eval/
security/
reference-repos/

# Test suites. They are a merge gate, not a runtime dependency.
tests/

# Version control metadata.
.git/
